{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa0e8c3e963b9cdb","type":"tool_result","content":"1:# Component: rhods-operator\n14:**Short**: rhods-operator is the RHOAI platform operator that orchestrates the lifecycle of all OpenShift AI components, managing their deployment, configuration, monitoring, and ingress through a set of custom resources and reconciliation controllers. [source: cmd/main.go:293, internal/controller/services/auth/auth_controller.go:57, internal/controller/services/gateway/gateway_controller.go:41]\n16:**Detailed**: rhods-operator serves as the central control plane for Red Hat OpenShift AI (RHOAI). It defines and reconciles a hierarchy of custom resources—DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, and per-component CRDs such as DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, and KServe—that declaratively describe the desired state of the AI/ML platform. The operator deploys and manages each component's manifests via kustomize-based rendering and template actions, while a set of service controllers handle cross-cutting concerns: the Auth service controller manages RBAC group policies and watches for dependent namespaces (models-as-a-service, kuadrant-system), the Gateway service controller provisions a data-science gateway with kube-auth-proxy OIDC infrastructure and conditionally integrates with Istio (EnvoyFilter, DestinationRule) and OpenShift OAuth, and the Monitoring service controller deploys Prometheus proxies and collectors for platform-level observability. A companion cloudmanager binary handles cloud-provider Kubernetes engine resources (AWS, Azure, CoreWeave). The operator exposes 19 CRDs, serves admission webhooks on port 9443 with service-ca TLS, and reads the cluster APIServer TLS profile to align its own TLS configuration with the platform-wide security policy. [source: Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.konflux:56-59, cmd/main.go:480-494, internal/controller/services/auth/auth_controller.go:56-88, internal/controller/services/gateway/gateway_controller.go:40-109, pkg/tls/profile.go:151-162]\n20:rhods-operator follows a layered controller-runtime reconciliation architecture with three distinct controller tiers: a top-level DataScienceCluster controller that owns the platform lifecycle, per-component controllers (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator, Trainer, TrainingOperator, TrustyAI) that manage individual AI/ML workload components, and service controllers (Auth, Gateway, Monitoring) that handle cross-cutting platform infrastructure.\n42:## Architecture Components\n44:| Component | Type | Purpose |\n50:| component-codegen | Go executable | Go entrypoint |\n70:| components.platform.opendatahub.io | v1alpha1 | DataSciencePipelines | Cluster | Core API | Custom resource managed by rhods-operator |\n71:| components.platform.opendatahub.io | v1alpha1 | Kueue | Cluster | Core API | Custom resource managed by rhods-operator |\n72:| components.platform.opendatahub.io | v1alpha1 | ModelRegistry | Cluster | Core API | Custom resource managed by rhods-operator |\n73:| components.platform.opendatahub.io | v1alpha1 | Ray | Cluster | Core API | Custom resource managed by rhods-operator |\n74:| components.platform.opendatahub.io | v1alpha1 | SparkOperator | Cluster | Core API | Custom resource managed by rhods-operator |\n75:| components.platform.opendatahub.io | v1alpha1 | Trainer | Cluster | Core API | Custom resource managed by rhods-operator |\n76:| components.platform.opendatahub.io | v1alpha1 | TrainingOperator | Cluster | Core API | Custom resource managed by rhods-operator |\n77:| components.platform.opendatahub.io | v1alpha1 | TrustyAI | Cluster | Core API | Custom resource managed by rhods-operator |\n---\n16:**Detailed**: odh-dashboard is a multi-component platform console consisting of three tiers: (1) a React/PatternFly single-page application served via Webpack Module Federation that hosts domain-specific UI modules; (2) a Node.js Fastify backend that proxies Kubernetes API requests using user impersonation and serves static assets behind kube-rbac-proxy; and (3) a Go operator (dashboard-operator) built on controller-runtime v0.23.3 that reconciles Dashboard custom resources, managing the lifecycle of the console deployment including RBAC, routes, and sidecar BFF services. The federated architecture includes ten Go BFF (backend-for-frontend) sidecars — core-bff, gen-ai, model-registry, mlflow, eval-hub, maas, agent-ops, automl, autorag, and data-registry — each serving domain-specific API endpoints on dedicated TLS-encrypted ports with Bearer token authentication. The operator watches platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) to reflect platform state in the dashboard and manages Gateway API HTTPRoutes for request routing. A bundled Workspace controller reconciles kubeflow.org/v1beta1 Workspace and WorkspaceKind resources with validating and conversion webhooks. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:52, Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:37, dashboard-operator/charts/dashboard/values.yaml:15-18, manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, dashboard-operator/go.mod:6-13]\n20:The odh-dashboard follows a micro-frontend architecture using Webpack Module Federation, where a host React application dynamically loads domain-specific UI modules (gen-ai, model-registry, notebooks, eval-hub, agent-ops, automl, autorag, data-registry, maas, mlflow) at runtime via `/_mf/:name/*` paths. Each UI module is paired with a dedicated Go BFF sidecar that handles its backend operations, creating a vertically-sliced architecture where each domain owns its full request path from UI through BFF to Kubernetes API. The federation configuration (in `manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml`) maps path prefixes to sidecar ports, enabling a single ingress point to fan out to domain-specific backends. [source: manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, backend/src/routes/module-federation.ts:1]\n24:Authentication follows a two-layer model: kube-rbac-proxy fronts the Node.js backend (port 8443) performing OpenShift OAuth validation and injecting identity headers (`x-forwarded-access-token`, `x-auth-request-user`, `x-auth-request-groups`), while each Go BFF sidecar independently validates Bearer tokens and performs SubjectAccessReviews against the Kubernetes API for fine-grained authorization. The agent-ops BFF extends this with per-agent RBAC via SubjectAccessReviews for agent-specific access control. The Node.js backend additionally proxies Kubernetes API calls (`/api/k8s/*`) by converting user tokens to impersonation headers, allowing the frontend to interact with the cluster API through the dashboard service boundary. [source: backend/src/utils/constants.ts:18-20, backend/src/utils/proxy.ts:39-42, packages/agent-ops/bff/internal/api/middleware.go:1]\n47:| Dockerfile.konflux.agent-ops:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n63:| agent-ops BFF | Go BFF Sidecar | Federated agent-ops backend-for-frontend service |\n87:| packages/agent-ops/Dockerfile.workspace:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n88:| packages/agent-ops/Dockerfile:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n129:| Name | Kind | API Group | Version | Scope | Supported Model Formats | Container Images | Built-in Adapter | Source |\n134:| Path | Method | Port | Protocol | Transport | Encryption | Auth | Owner | Purpose |\n141:| /agent-ops/api/* | ALL | 8843 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | user_token |  | Agent-Ops BFF API |\n142:| /agent-ops/api/v1/* | ALL | 8843 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | user_token |  | Agent-Ops BFF API |\n146:| /autorag/api/* | ALL | 8743 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | user_token |  | Autorag BFF API |\n147:| /autorag/api/v1/* | ALL | 8743 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | user_token |  | Autorag BFF API |\n159:| /mod-arch/api/* | ALL | 8843 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | user_token |  | Agent-Ops BFF API |\n160:| /mod-arch/api/v1/* | ALL | 8843 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | user_token |  | Agent-Ops BFF API |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b09d530114d28c8","agent_id":"a8a4c6b9da1a738b7","session_id":"ec1d2a2a-7323-4c5d-bb4c-fc6cac99ed4f","uuid":"d327ff3f-1070-474d-925d-73db8420c7ac","timestamp":"2026-10-22T09:50:36.848Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa0e8c3e963b9cdb","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8006 decomposition"}
