ings (E007/E008 are distinct images;\n126\tE011/E017 add disjoint CSV entries; E014/E018 cover disjoint doc content). No work-product\n127\tmixing: docs-authoring work is separated from code (E014, E018) and the branch separates\n128\twiring (E016) from its documentation (E018).\n129\t\n130\tHowever, E010 is clearly oversized. The strategy's own effort estimate prices the\n131\trhods-operator component-controller workstream at 2-3 sprints (component CRD definition, DSC\n132\tschema extension, controller action pipeline, registry registration, kustomize manifest\n133\tintegration, full Managed/Removed lifecycle, manifest overlays) — and even with testing moved\n134\tto E013 and CSV packaging moved to E011, E010 still carries multiple sprints of work in a\n135\tsingle epic, well beyond the ~2-week epic guideline. A split is feasible along the\n136\tdeploy-vs-Removed-cleanup axis: the strategy itself treats cleanup semantics as a separable\n137\tconcern with its own Investigation (E005), its own risk row, and its own NFR. Major issue —\n138\tscore 0.\n139\t\n140\t### 4. Type Correctness (1/2)\n141\t\n142\tAll six Investigations genuinely change downstream structure (manifest baseline rewrite,\n143\tvariant-set branch, Keycloak configuration, dashboard scope rewrite, cleanup semantics, OTEL\n144\trequirement branch) and each has downstream dependents; all Implementations produce concrete\n145\tartifacts. Every `gated_by` target appears in the epic's direct `dependencies`, and every\n146\tpopulated `gate_failure_impact` has both `action` and `fallback_approach` describing a real\n147\tchange on gate failure.\n148\t\n149\tGating-metadata gap (two instances of the same finding): `gated_by` is under-populated\n150\trelative to the Investigation dependencies.\n151\t\n152\t- E010 depends on Investigations E001, E003, and E005 but gates only on E001. Its own\n153\t  acceptance criteria call the Keycloak/ClientRegistrationController resolution and the\n154\t  cleanup-semantics resolution \"decision gates\" the epic \"is blocked on\" — yet neither is\n155\t  recorded in `gated_by`, so automated consumers keying on that field will miss two of the\n156\t  three gates.\n157\t- E014 depends on Investigations E001 and E003 but gates only on E003, while its AC calls the\n158\t  upstream-validation outcome a decision gate for the documentation content.\n159\t\n160\tThe gate substance is otherwise modeled (DAG edges, prose, ACs, and a populated\n161\t`gate_failure_impact` on the primary gate), so this is a metadata-completeness issue rather\n162\tthan a structural one — score 1.\n163\t\n164\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n165\t\n166\tType-appropriate signal sets throughout: `investigation_signals` on E001-E006, `ai_signals` on\n167\tall Implementation epics, no mixing; all 18 `-ai-signals.md` files present. Investigation\n168\tvalues check out against the 5-signal rubric: `human_judgment_required: -2` where the gating\n169\tquestion is itself a PM/UX/team decision (E002, E004, E006); `source_accessibility: 0` where\n170\tthe answer is an unresolved discussion rather than a readable artifact; `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1`\n171\twhere only peripheral validation needs a live cluster (E001, E003, E005). Implementation\n172\tvalues are largely consistent with the strategy and architecture context (E010's and E012's\n173\tclaims verified against rhods-operator.md and odh-dashboard.md).\n174\t\n175\tOne rationale-value mismatch: E008 scores `open_questions: 0`, but its written rationale\n176\targues the variant-set question \"does not change this epic's proxy-only approach\" — which is\n177\tthe argument for +1, and is the same logic twin epic E007 uses to score +1. The defensible\n178\tjustification for 0 (the kagenti-extensions midstream fork's existence is only assumed per\n179\thealth warning 5 and is verified by E001) appears solely under `repo_access`. The value is\n180\tdefensible; the rationale is misattributed/thin — score 1.\n181\t\n182\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (0/2)\n183\t\n184\tACs are otherwise specific and testable: Given/When/Then with kubectl-measurable outcomes on\n185\tE010; decision gates expressed as ACs; digest-availability configuration-reference gates per\n186\tRule 13; no epic body references sibling epics by draft ID. Docs epics carry the Rule 24\n187\ttechnical-review AC (E014, E018). Konflux epics carry green-build ACs (E007, E008,\n188\tBRANCH-B-E015), and E010 carries \"Build pipeline green for rhods-operator changes (Konflux)\".\n189\t\n190\tBut two epics that change Konflux-built repositories are missing the Rule 25 \"build pipeline\n191\tgreen\" AC:\n192\t\n193\t- E011 adds `RELATED_IMAGE_*` entries plus the KagentiOperator CRD/RBAC declarations to the\n194\t  rhods-operator ClusterServiceVersion — the same repository as E010 and the same work class\n195\t  as branch twin BRANCH-B-E017, both of which carry \"Build pipeline green for rhods-operator\n196\t  changes (Konflux)\". E011 has three ACs, none about the build pipeline.\n197\t- E012 implements a new Go BFF container and feature flag in odh-dashboard — a Konflux-built\n198\t  repository per architecture context (Dockerfile.konflux, per-BFF Dockerfile.konflux.*\n199\t  files) — and has no build-pipeline AC at all.\n200\t\n201\tMultiple missing rule-mandated ACs — major, score 0. The rule applies to every epic that\n202\tmeets its criteria, not just the most obvious one.\n203\t\n204\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n205\t\n206\tFull scope coverage: every strategy workstream maps to epics (Konflux onboarding E007/E008\n207\tplus BRANCH-B-E015, component controller E010, CSV packaging E011 plus BRANCH-B-E017,\n208\tdashboard E012, QE E013, docs E014 plus BRANCH-B-E018); all six strategy open questions have\n209\tcorresponding Investigations; the open parent-RFE OTEL MUST is modeled as Investigation E006\n210\twith conditional wiring (BRANCH-B-E016) and documentation (BRANCH-B-E018) branches covering\n211\tboth bounded outcomes. Risks are carried into epic scope/ACs (Konflux lead time on E007,\n212\twebhook ordering on E013, port collision on E012, orphaned resources on E005/E010/E013).\n213\tDocumentation Support: Yes is honored with docs-authoring epic E014 (Rule 11). Out-of-scope\n214\titems are consistently fenced to RHAIRFE-7587/7588/7589/7408, with intentional gaps flagged in\n215\thealth warnings rather than silently dropped. Skipping repo-onboarding for the new components\n216\tis justified by the strategy's dependency table (midstream fork status \"Exists\") and backstopped\n217\tby E001's fork-readiness verification with a documented remediation path.\n218\t\n219\tMinor: E008's scope omits the container-security NFR conventions (non-root USER 65532,\n220\tcapability dropping, seccompProfile.type: RuntimeDefault) that E007 explicitly carries,\n221\talthough the NFR applies to all Go operator/sidecar images; E012's new BFF image build wiring\n222\t(Dockerfile.konflux pattern) is implicit in \"following the established federated-module\n223\tpattern\" rather than explicit scope.\n224\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-be364346b92c0a7e","agent_id":"a96bcd1a69cb8e30f","session_id":"ec1d2a2a-7323-4c5d-bb4c-fc6cac99ed4f","uuid":"d0913a74-b062-47ef-b91a-ad9c4c9292ad","timestamp":"2026-10-22T10:00:59.343Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-827c9debd0ec586b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Second revise RHAISTRAT-8006"}
