{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"Exit code 1\n16:**Detailed**: odh-dashboard is a multi-component platform console consisting of three tiers: (1) a React/PatternFly single-page application served via Webpack Module Federation that hosts domain-specific UI modules; (2) a Node.js Fastify backend that proxies Kubernetes API requests using user impersonation and serves static assets behind kube-rbac-proxy; and (3) a Go operator (dashboard-operator) built on controller-runtime v0.23.3 that reconciles Dashboard custom resources, managing the lifecycle of the console deployment including RBAC, routes, and sidecar BFF services. The federated architecture includes ten Go BFF (backend-for-frontend) sidecars — core-bff, gen-ai, model-registry, mlflow, eval-hub, maas, agent-ops, automl, autorag, and data-registry — each serving domain-specific API endpoints on dedicated TLS-encrypted ports with Bearer token authentication. The operator watches platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) to reflect platform state in the dashboard and manages Gateway API HTTPRoutes for request routing. A bundled Workspace controller reconciles kubeflow.org/v1beta1 Workspace and WorkspaceKind resources with validating and conversion webhooks. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:52, Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:37, dashboard-operator/charts/dashboard/values.yaml:15-18, manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, dashboard-operator/go.mod:6-13]\n20:The odh-dashboard follows a micro-frontend architecture using Webpack Module Federation, where a host React application dynamically loads domain-specific UI modules (gen-ai, model-registry, notebooks, eval-hub, agent-ops, automl, autorag, data-registry, maas, mlflow) at runtime via `/_mf/:name/*` paths. Each UI module is paired with a dedicated Go BFF sidecar that handles its backend operations, creating a vertically-sliced architecture where each domain owns its full request path from UI through BFF to Kubernetes API. The federation configuration (in `manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml`) maps path prefixes to sidecar ports, enabling a single ingress point to fan out to domain-specific backends. [source: manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, backend/src/routes/module-federation.ts:1]\n24:Authentication follows a two-layer model: kube-rbac-proxy fronts the Node.js backend (port 8443) performing OpenShift OAuth validation and injecting identity headers (`x-forwarded-access-token`, `x-auth-request-user`, `x-auth-request-groups`), while each Go BFF sidecar independently validates Bearer tokens and performs SubjectAccessReviews against the Kubernetes API for fine-grained authorization. The agent-ops BFF extends this with per-agent RBAC via SubjectAccessReviews for agent-specific access control. The Node.js backend additionally proxies Kubernetes API calls (`/api/k8s/*`) by converting user tokens to impersonation headers, allowing the frontend to interact with the cluster API through the dashboard service boundary. [source: backend/src/utils/constants.ts:18-20, backend/src/utils/proxy.ts:39-42, packages/agent-ops/bff/internal/api/middleware.go:1]\n47:| Dockerfile.konflux.agent-ops:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n63:| agent-ops BFF | Go BFF Sidecar | Federated agent-ops backend-for-frontend service |\n87:| packages/agent-ops/Dockerfile.workspace:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n88:| packages/agent-ops/Dockerfile:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n136:| / | GET | 8443 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS (kube-rbac-proxy) | OpenShift project list |  | Serve React SPA |\n138:| /_bff/mlflow/api/* | ALL | 8343 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | user_token |  | Mlflow BFF API |\n139:| /_bff/mlflow/api/v1/* | ALL | 8343 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | user_token |  | Mlflow BFF API |\n140:| /_mf/:name/* | ALL | 8443 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS (kube-rbac-